-- allium: 3
-- log-processor-lifecycle.allium
--
-- THIS SPEC RUNS AHEAD OF THE IMPLEMENTATION.
--   Where it and the code disagree, the spec is the target; db.allium's fencing
--   sections describe the code as it stands.
--
-- == What this specifies ==
--
--   Which node leads a database, and how leadership changes hands. Every node
--   follows every database's replica log; a follower may claim leadership, and the
--   log's own ordering decides whether the claim succeeds. The leader is the sole
--   writer of that log.
--
-- == The mechanism, end to end ==
--
--   1. Every node reads the replica log and remembers the highest term in it.
--   2. A follower that has read its log over a randomised timeout and found nothing
--      appends a no-op one term above that, and is told the position it landed at.
--      That is its claim.
--   3. The claim confers leadership iff no record BEFORE it carries that term or
--      higher. Rivals at the same term are settled by position; the earliest wins.
--   4. The claimant reads on to its own claim. If nothing it read in the meantime
--      carried its term or higher, it has won -- and by then it holds the prefix.
--   5. A leader stamps its term on everything it appends, and acknowledges a write
--      only on reading it back at its own term. Reading back a HIGHER term means it
--      has been superseded, so it resigns.
--
-- == One stopwatch ==
--
--   Everything timed here is timed off a single quantity: how long this participant
--   has been reading its log and finding nothing (quiet_for, section 2).
--   Three thresholds read it -- claim, give up on a claim, resign -- and each is a named predicate over it rather than a clock of its own (QuietIsMeasuredAcrossReads, section 5).
--   A participant that is not being delivered to cannot advance any of the three thresholds, so being unfed is not a route to claiming.
--
-- == Sections ==
--
--    1. The log we elect over       what it is, and what it must guarantee
--    2. The node's role in one database  the three states, and what each one holds
--    3. Reading the log            the one reader, and everything that keys off it
--    4. The term                   where it comes from, and why it only climbs
--    5. Claiming                   the claim, the adjudication, and losing
--    6. Taking over                the pipeline swap, and its failure
--    7. Leading, and standing down  asserting leadership, and the ways out
--    8. What the node may rely on  every property group, in one place
--    9. What the deployment must supply  the obligations election cannot self-enforce
--   10. Open questions
--
-- == Excludes ==
--
--   Live-index internals, record processing, block lifecycle, the log's wire
--   protocol, the ExternalSource lifecycle.
--   live-index.allium treats WHICH role is in play as a given; this spec is that gap.
--   Reference it where relevant rather than duplicating it.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. The log we elect over
--
-- The replica log, its records, and the guarantees election needs from them.
-- Election makes each of those guarantees load-bearing for SAFETY, where a follower
-- alone needed them only for durability.
------------------------------------------------------------

value Position {
    -- A position in the replica log's total order.
    offset: Integer
}

external entity ReplicaRecord {
    -- Every record carries a term: of the leader that appended it, or of the claim
    -- that it is. See: db.allium for the record kinds.
    --   This is what makes the log self-describing about leadership -- a reader needs
    --   nothing but the records to work out who leads.
    log: ReplicaLog
    position: Position
    term: Integer

    -- Derived: this record, read as a claim, conferred leadership -- no record before
    -- it carries its term or higher.
    --   At-or-above, not above: two nodes claiming the same term are settled by position, and the earliest wins.
    --   The adjudication is a property of the log rather than of any reader, so every reader reaches the same verdict.
    --   No participant evaluates it in this form; it would have to retain the terms of records it read long ago.
    --   See TheLocalTestDecidesTheSameVerdict.
    confers: not log.records.any(r =>
        r.position.offset < position.offset and r.term >= term)
}

external entity ReplicaLog {
    -- A durable, totally ordered sequence that every node reads and only the leader
    -- writes. See: db.allium.
    database: Database
    records: ReplicaRecord with log = this
}

contract DurableTotalOrder {
    -- What the log must supply for election over it to be sound.
    append: (log: ReplicaLog, term: Integer) -> Position
    read_from: (log: ReplicaLog, after: Position) -> List<ReplicaRecord>

    @invariant AppendReturnsItsOwnPosition
        -- An accepted append tells its author the position the record now occupies.
        --   So a claimant knows, without consulting anyone, which prefix its claim
        --   falls to be adjudicated against. This is why adjudication is positional
        --   and the record needs no identifier of its own.

    @invariant OneAcceptedAppendYieldsOneRecord
        -- An accepted append puts exactly one record in the log; a refused one puts
        -- none, and the author is told which happened.
        --   An append that landed twice would leave a second claim at the same term
        --   behind the author's own, which it would then adjudicate itself the loser
        --   of. An append whose outcome cannot be established leaves a claim nobody
        --   owns: it fences the incumbent, and no one takes over from it.

    @invariant OneOrderSeenByEveryReader
        -- All readers of a log see the same records at the same positions.
        --   The property a quorum protocol would otherwise have to manufacture. Two
        --   readers that disagreed about the order could disagree about who won.

    @invariant DeliveryFollowsPositionOrder
        -- A reader is delivered records in position order, and reaching a position
        -- means having been delivered everything before it.
        --   OneOrderSeenByEveryReader fixes where each record sits; this fixes the order a reader meets them in, and the two are independent.
        --   Without it, reading one's own claim back is no longer evidence of holding the prefix, and AConferredClaimantHoldsThePrefix has nothing under it.
        --   A read that would skip a position has to fail rather than proceed.
        --   A claimant silently handed a gap finds its own claim unopposed and concludes it won an election it lost.

    @invariant QuietIsReported
        -- A read that found nothing says so, rather than blocking until something
        -- arrives.
        --   The only evidence the log is quiet, so it is the only thing an election
        --   can start on. A reader that never reports an empty read disables the
        --   mechanism outright -- silently, and on every node at once. See
        --   AnIdleLogStillTicks for the rate it has to happen at.

    @invariant AcceptedRecordsAreNeverWithdrawn
        -- Once an append is accepted, its record is never removed, replaced or moved
        -- while it remains above the retention boundary.
        --   Ordinarily a durability property; here a safety one, because withdrawing
        --   a claim after another node has read it would let two nodes reach opposite
        --   verdicts on the same election. The deployment has to be configured for it
        --   and not merely trusted -- see DurabilityComesFromDeployedConfiguration.

    @invariant RetentionKeepsEverythingAboveTheBoundary
        -- Records at or after the persisted boundary's position are retained;
        -- reclaiming those below it is expected.
        --   This is what puts a floor under the term, so that no claim is ever
        --   adjudicated against a prefix cut away beneath it.
        --   The records a claimant's verdict turns on sit above the boundary it
        --   resumed from, and it holds its claim for at most claim_timeout before
        --   abandoning it (AbandonOverdueClaim, section 5) -- so the window in which
        --   reclamation could change a verdict under it is bounded by that timeout,
        --   which is orders of magnitude below any retention horizon worth
        --   configuring.
}

actor Log {
    -- The replica log: a party with obligations, not a driver of role changes.
    --   It decides who leads only in the sense that its ordering is the evidence.
    identified_by: Database where name != ""
}

surface ReplicaLogBoundary {
    -- Everything a participant needs from its log, and the sole channel by which it
    -- learns about leadership.
    --   Every role change traces back to a read here -- one that found a record, or
    --   one that found nothing.
    facing log: Log

    context participant: Participant

    provides:
        -- One record delivered to this participant, whichever role it holds.
        RecordObserved(log, participant, record)

        -- A read that had nothing to deliver.
        LogReadFoundNothing(log, participant)

    contracts:
        demands DurableTotalOrder

    @guarantee ElectionNeedsNothingBeyondAnOrderedLog
        -- Append, be told the append's own position, and read forward in order --
        -- including being told when a read found nothing. That is all.
        --   A participant is never asked how far the log extends beyond what it has been delivered, only whether a read brought anything back.
        --   So any durable ordered log can back leadership, whether or not it offers an election mechanism of its own.

    @guarantee TheLogDoesNotInitiateRoleChanges
        -- The log never asks a participant to lead or to stand down, and is told
        -- nothing about which role it holds.
        --   It delivers records; the participant draws every conclusion. So there is
        --   no state outside this spec that could disagree about who leads.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. The node's role in one database
--
-- The participant -- one node's participation in one database's leadership -- the
-- three states it moves between, and the pipelines that back them.
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity NodeIdentity {
    -- The node hosting a participant. What distinguishes one participant in a
    -- database from another, which is the situation the whole spec is about.
    name: String
}

external entity Database {
    -- The database whose replica log this node follows or leads. See: db.allium.
    --   Its lifecycle governs the participant: tearing the database down tears the
    --   role and any in-flight claim down with it, so there is no resignation on
    --   shutdown.
    name: String
    log: ReplicaLog

    -- Where a participant seeds its term and read position from. See section 4.
    boundary: PersistedBoundary

    -- Whether the deployment permits this node to lead this database. See section 9.
    may_lead: Boolean
}

external entity FollowerPipeline {
    -- A running follower: consumes the replica log to build local queryable state,
    -- and is the sole replica-log consumer for its node. See: live-index.allium.
    --   Where it starts, not where it has got to.
    --   The participant is the authority for how far its node has read (replica_position, below).
    resume_from: Position
}

external entity LeaderPipeline {
    -- A running leader: consumes the source log, resolves client transactions and
    -- appends to the replica log. See: live-index.allium (Leader variant).
    --   The term it stamps on every record it appends. The fence built on that stamp
    --   is ExactlyOneLeaderIsEverAcknowledged, in section 7.
    term: Integer

    resume_from: Position
}

entity Participant {
    -- One node's participation in one database's leadership.
    --   Every node has one per database it hosts, and it starts out following.
    node: NodeIdentity
    database: Database
    log: ReplicaLog

    -- following  running a follower; no claim outstanding.
    -- claiming   a claim is in the log and the participant has not yet read it back,
    --            so the verdict is not yet known.
    -- leading    running a leader; the sole writer of this log.
    state: following | claiming | leading

    transitions state {
        following -> claiming    -- claimed leadership (section 5)
        claiming -> following    -- claim lost or given up (section 5), or taking over
                                 -- failed (section 6)
        claiming -> leading      -- claim read back, conferring, took over (section 6)
        leading -> following     -- resigned or stood down (section 7)
        -- No terminal state: a participant cycles indefinitely.
    }

    -- The highest term this participant has seen in its log. See section 4.
    --   Held for the participant's whole life: seeded once from the persisted boundary, and raised as records are read, whichever role reads them.
    highest_term_seen: Integer

    -- How far this node has read its replica log, whichever role it held while doing
    -- so. One reader per node, one position, surviving every role change.
    replica_position: Position

    -- When this participant last finished reading its log, and when it last found a
    -- record there. The gap between them is how long the LOG has been quiet, which is
    -- the only thing any of this spec's timing may rest on -- see
    -- QuietIsMeasuredAcrossReads.
    last_read_at: Timestamp
    last_record_at: Timestamp

    -- Redrawn from the configured range whenever the participant waits afresh.
    --   Randomising it keeps two followers from claiming together REPEATEDLY. It does
    --   not stop them claiming together once, which adjudication handles.
    election_timeout: Duration

    -- How many claims in a row this participant abandoned without reaching a verdict.
    --   Widens the range the next timeout is drawn from
    --   (ALoserCannotStarveTheElection, section 5). Back to zero as soon as a claim
    --   reaches a verdict, winning or losing: losing means the claim came back, which
    --   is evidence that reads are arriving, and arriving reads are the only thing
    --   being backed away from here.
    abandoned_claims: Integer

    -- When this leader last appended.
    --   Rate-limits its assertions independently of whether anything is coming back,
    --   so a leader whose reads have stopped does not assert on every empty read.
    last_appended_at: Timestamp when state = leading

    follower: FollowerPipeline when state = following | claiming
    claim: Claim when state = claiming
    leader: LeaderPipeline when state = leading

    -- Derived: how long the log has been quiet, as this participant has observed it.
    --   Measured between two of its OWN reads rather than against the current time,
    --   so it advances only while the participant is actually looking and finding
    --   nothing. Time spent processing a record does not accumulate here.
    --   The one stopwatch. The three predicates below are thresholds over it, one per
    --   role, and nothing else in this spec is timed.
    quiet_for: last_read_at - last_record_at

    -- Derived: the log has been quiet long enough to justify an election.
    quiet_long_enough: quiet_for >= election_timeout

    -- Derived: this claimant has been reading long enough that its own claim should
    -- have come back.
    claim_overdue: quiet_for >= config.claim_timeout

    -- Derived: this leader has been reading long enough that its own appends should
    -- have come back.
    read_back_stalled: quiet_for >= config.acknowledgement_timeout
}

invariant ARoleIsBackedByItsPipeline {
    -- Whatever role the participant holds, it holds the matching pipeline.
    --   The structural half of NoRoleWithoutALivePipeline (section 6): that the pipeline is RUNNING is temporal, and lives there.
    for p in Participants:
        (p.state in {following, claiming} implies p.follower != null)
        and (p.state = claiming implies p.claim != null)
        and (p.state = leading implies p.leader != null)
}

invariant AParticipantFollowsItsOwnDatabasesLog {
    for p in Participants:
        p.log = p.database.log
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. Reading the log
--
-- One reader per node, and the two outcomes of a read: it found a record, or it found
-- nothing. Every rule in sections 5 to 7 keys off one or the other, and off nothing
-- else -- a participant that is not reading learns nothing and concludes nothing.
------------------------------------------------------------

contract SingleLogReader {
    @invariant OneReplicaConsumerPerNode
        -- One reader of the replica log per node, whichever role it holds.
        --   A follower reads it to build local state; a leader reads it back as part
        --   of its own append cycle. So there is never a second opinion about the
        --   highest term seen or the position reached.
}

rule ObserveRecord {
    -- The single point at which a participant learns anything from its log.
    when: RecordObserved(log, participant, record)

    -- What was known immediately BEFORE this record. A claim's verdict turns on the
    -- prefix strictly before it, so the claim's own record has to be adjudicated
    -- against this rather than against a term that has already absorbed it.
    let term_before = participant.highest_term_seen

    ensures:
        participant.highest_term_seen = max(participant.highest_term_seen, record.term)
        participant.replica_position = record.position

        -- Any record at all defers this participant's election, which is why a leader
        -- only has to assert leadership when it has nothing else to write.
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now

    -- Every conclusion about leadership is drawn from a record, so they all hang off
    -- this one emission and each states its own condition. The consumers are
    -- AbandonLostClaim (section 5), TakeLeadership (section 6) and
    -- ResignOnSupersession (section 7).
    ensures: RecordProcessed(participant: participant, record: record, term_before: term_before)
}

rule ObserveNothing {
    -- A read that came back with nothing, which is the only evidence the log is quiet.
    --   Nothing is learned about leadership here and no term changes. What advances is
    --   the participant's account of how long it has been looking without finding --
    --   and only a read can advance that, never the passage of time.
    when: LogReadFoundNothing(log, participant)

    ensures:
        participant.last_read_at = now
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4. The term
--
-- The term is what fences a superseded leader, so everything depends on it never
-- going backwards. Two guarantees deliver that: durable below, monotonic above.
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity PersistedBoundary {
    -- The most recent durably persisted boundary, written by whichever node led at
    -- the time, carrying the term and replica position then in force.
    --   So the term is durable in its own right, and survives the loss of every
    --   process that has ever held it. See: db.allium (the block catalog, which owns
    --   when a boundary is written).
    term: Integer
    position: Position
}

contract TermDiscipline {
    @invariant TheTermIsDurableBelowAndMonotonicAbove
        -- A participant seeds its term from the persisted boundary and thereafter only
        -- ever raises it.
        --   Nothing restarting can put it below a term already established, and nothing
        --   running can lower it, so no leader claims a term an earlier leader used.
        --   It is a property of each participant's own history, not a standing
        --   comparison against the boundary. The boundary is shared and advances with
        --   whichever node leads, so a follower that is merely behind sits below it as
        --   a matter of course.
}

invariant ALeaderLeadsAtARecordedTerm {
    -- A leader's term is never above what the participant has read back.
    --   It takes over only after reading its own claim, so a leader's term is always
    --   one the log has recorded, never one it merely intends.
    for p in Participants:
        p.state = leading implies p.leader.term <= p.highest_term_seen
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 5. Claiming
--
-- The claim, the positional rule that adjudicates it, and what happens when it
-- loses.
------------------------------------------------------------

value Claim {
    -- One node's bid for leadership: a record it appended, carrying the term it
    -- claims.
    --   Its position is its identity, which is why nothing further has to be written
    --   into the record to tell claims apart, and why a participant holding at most
    --   one at a time needs no token to check a verdict against.
    term: Integer
    position: Position
}

invariant ConferringRecordsAreTermOrdered {
    -- Read along a log, the records that conferred leadership carry strictly
    -- increasing terms.
    --   Follows from the adjudication: a later record only confers if nothing before it held its term or higher, so two claims at the same term cannot both confer.
    --   This is what lets a leader treat a higher term read back as proof it has been superseded.
    --   It holds of records whose authors are long gone.
    for a in ReplicaRecords:
        for b in ReplicaRecords:
            a.log = b.log and a.confers and b.confers
                and a.position.offset < b.position.offset
                implies a.term < b.term
}

contract ClaimDiscipline {
    @invariant TheLocalTestDecidesTheSameVerdict
        -- A claimant decides the adjudication from the highest term it had seen
        -- immediately before reading its own claim back: it won iff that term is below
        -- its claim's.
        --   Equivalent to the positional rule (ReplicaRecord.confers), not an approximation of it.
        --   The records before the claim divide into those the claimant had read when it appended -- all below its term, since it claimed one above the highest it had seen -- and those it read in between, which this test examines directly.
        --   A test against a running highest-term-seen that has already absorbed the claimant's own record finds every same-term rival conferring, or none.

    @invariant AConferredClaimantHoldsThePrefix
        -- A claimant that has reached a verdict has read every record before its
        -- claim.
        --   Reaching a verdict means reading its own claim back, which means having
        --   read the whole prefix (DeliveryFollowsPositionOrder). So leader
        --   completeness -- the new leader holds everything its predecessors wrote --
        --   is the same act as adjudicating, and the claimant owes no separate
        --   catch-up before it may lead.
        --   It is also why the wait is short. The claim was made because the log had
        --   been quiet for a whole election timeout, so there is next to nothing ahead
        --   of the claim to read; a log with a backlog defers the election instead of
        --   producing a claim, because every record read resets the clock.

    @invariant ALostClaimReturnsToFollowing
        -- A participant whose claim lost returns to following, available for reads and
        -- for a later claim.
        --   Contested elections are ordinary; the database stays available through one.

    @invariant QuietIsMeasuredAcrossReads
        -- Every timeout here measures the LOG being quiet, across the participant's
        -- own reads, never the participant being busy.
        --   All three thresholds advance only across reads that came back with nothing.
        --   Time spent processing a record teaches a participant nothing about anyone's health, so its own slowness must not turn into a conclusion.
        --   Violated, the participant's slowness becomes a verdict on somebody else's health, in both directions: a slow follower claims and wins, evicting a leader that was healthy throughout, and a slow leader resigns, discarding writes the log had accepted.
        --   A participant that is not being delivered to cannot reach any of the three thresholds, because nothing advances them -- so being unfed is not a route to claiming.

    @invariant ALoserCannotStarveTheElection
        -- A participant that keeps claiming and never wins cannot stop the others
        -- electing.
        --   It matters because a claim is a record, and every record read resets every
        --   participant's stopwatch. So a node claiming in a tight loop would hold all
        --   its peers permanently short of their own timeouts, and a database whose
        --   leader had genuinely died would never get another one -- the failure mode
        --   is total, and caused by a node that is merely wrong rather than malicious.
        --   Two things bound it. A claimant holds its claim until claim_timeout before
        --   abandoning it, and claim_timeout exceeds election_timeout_max, so it can
        --   inject at most one record per claim and always leaves a quiet window longer
        --   than any peer's timeout. And an abandoned claim widens the range the next
        --   timeout is drawn from (AbandonOverdueClaim, this section), so several such
        --   nodes interleaving thin out rather than compounding.
}

config {
    -- The values are a starting point. What the spec fixes is the RELATIONS between
    -- them -- here, that the floor is generous enough to absorb ordinary scheduling
    -- and delivery delay, so a healthy leader's assertion always lands first
    -- (AssertionOutpacesTheElectionTimeout, section 7).

    -- The range a follower's election timeout is drawn from.
    --   The spread between them has to be comfortably wider than the interval at which
    --   empty reads are reported (AnIdleLogStillTicks, section 9). The stopwatch only
    --   advances on a read, so two followers whose draws differ by less than one
    --   reporting interval tip over on the same read however far apart their timeouts
    --   nominally are, and the randomisation buys nothing.
    election_timeout_min: Duration = 6.seconds
    election_timeout_max: Duration = 12.seconds

    -- How long a claimant keeps reading for its own claim before giving up on it.
    --   Generous relative to the election timeout: the claim is one record on a log
    --   that had been quiet, so the ordinary case is immediate.
    --   It MUST exceed election_timeout_max: that relation is what stops a claimant whose reads are not arriving from resetting its peers' stopwatches faster than they can run down (ALoserCannotStarveTheElection, above).
    claim_timeout: Duration = 30.seconds

    -- How far the election-timeout range widens after a claim is abandoned.
    --   The drawn range widens by this factor again for each successive abandonment, so a participant that cannot reach a verdict backs away from the log instead of returning on the same cadence.
    abandon_backoff_factor: Integer = 2
}

rule ClaimLeadership {
    -- The log has been quiet long enough, and this node may lead and is caught up.
    --   Claiming is one append.
    when: participant: Participant.quiet_long_enough

    -- Also guards re-firing: the participant leaves `following` as it claims, and
    -- returns only with a fresh timeout and a quiet_for back at zero.
    requires: participant.state = following

    -- Claiming is the only way into leadership, so eligibility is decided here and
    -- nowhere else.
    requires: participant.database.may_lead

    -- One above everything seen, so the claim is adjudicated against a prefix that
    -- cannot already contain its term.
    let claimed_term = participant.highest_term_seen + 1
    let claim_position = ClaimAppended(log: participant.log, term: claimed_term)

    ensures:
        participant.claim = Claim.created(
            term: claimed_term,
            position: claim_position
        )
        participant.state = claiming
}

rule HoldOffAfterFailedClaimAppend {
    -- The claim was refused, so nothing was written and there is nothing to
    -- adjudicate.
    --   The participant never left following and its stopwatch is still past the
    --   timeout, so without a fresh wait it re-claims on the next read and keeps doing
    --   so for as long as appends are refused. The wait also gives whatever refused the
    --   append time to recover.
    when: ClaimAppendFailed(node, participant)

    requires: participant.state = following

    ensures:
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now
        participant.election_timeout =
            RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min, config.election_timeout_max)
}

invariant OnlyEligibleParticipantsLead {
    -- A node the deployment excluded from leadership does not lead.
    --   Scoped to leading rather than to claiming: eligibility withdrawn from a
    --   claimant leaves a claim in the log that will still be adjudicated, so the
    --   participant takes over and then stands down (StandDownOnIneligibility,
    --   section 7) rather than leaving a conferring record nobody owns.
    for p in Participants:
        p.state = leading implies p.database.may_lead
}

rule AbandonLostClaim {
    -- Another node's claim got in first (ALostClaimReturnsToFollowing).
    --   The follower never stopped, so there is nothing to rebuild and reads are
    --   served throughout. A freshly drawn timeout before the next attempt is what
    --   stops a contested election repeating itself.
    when: RecordProcessed(participant, record, term_before)

    requires: participant.state = claiming
    requires: record.position.offset >= participant.claim.position.offset

    -- Something at or above this claim's term preceded it
    -- (TheLocalTestDecidesTheSameVerdict).
    requires: term_before >= participant.claim.term

    ensures:
        participant.claim = null
        participant.state = following
        participant.abandoned_claims = 0
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now
        participant.election_timeout =
            RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min, config.election_timeout_max)
}

rule AbandonOverdueClaim {
    -- The claimant has been reading and its own claim has not come back.
    --   Its reader is not delivering, so it cannot reach a verdict, and it must not
    --   hold the claiming state open on the strength of a prefix it is no longer being
    --   given. Returning to following is what lets it try again once reads resume.
    --   It cannot be left to another node to resolve. A deployment may have exactly one
    --   participant eligible to lead, and then nobody else will ever claim, however
    --   long the log stays quiet.
    when: participant: Participant.claim_overdue

    requires: participant.state = claiming

    -- Widened for each successive abandonment, so a participant that cannot reach a
    -- verdict returns to the log less and less often rather than on a fixed cadence.
    let abandonments = participant.abandoned_claims + 1
    let scale = 1 + config.abandon_backoff_factor * abandonments

    ensures:
        participant.claim = null
        participant.state = following
        participant.abandoned_claims = abandonments
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now
        participant.election_timeout =
            RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min * scale,
                          config.election_timeout_max * scale)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 6. Taking over
--
-- The claim conferred, and by reaching that verdict the claimant already holds the
-- prefix (AConferredClaimantHoldsThePrefix). All that remains is swapping the
-- follower for a leader -- and recovering if that fails partway.
------------------------------------------------------------

contract HandoverDiscipline {
    @invariant NoRoleWithoutALivePipeline
        -- At every observable point the role is backed by a RUNNING pipeline.
        --   A follower while following or claiming, a leader while leading -- never
        --   one that has been torn down.
        --   The swap necessarily stops the follower before the leader is running, so a
        --   failure in that window has to re-open a follower. Otherwise the
        --   participant is nominally following a pipeline that cannot advance, and
        --   since reading the log is the only way it learns anything, it can neither
        --   serve reads nor reach a verdict on a later claim.
        --   A pipeline that stops outside the swap is the same obligation arriving at another moment: a leader's stops the role (ResignOnPipelineFailure, section 7), and a follower's fails the database (FailDatabaseOnFollowerPipelineFailure, section 7), so no state is left backed by a pipeline that cannot advance.

    @invariant TheFollowerStopsBeforeTheLeaderStarts
        -- Nothing is processed twice across the swap.
        --   A previous leader may have left work owed at the point it stopped, which
        --   the incoming leader discharges with leader semantics before appending
        --   anything of its own. What that work is, and how it is recognised, belongs
        --   to the block lifecycle -- see: db.allium. What this spec fixes is the
        --   ordering: the follower stops first, and a failure anywhere in the sequence
        --   leaves the owed work still owed rather than recorded as done.
}

rule TakeLeadership {
    -- The claim came back, and it conferred leadership.
    --   Nothing from here on can change the verdict.
    when: RecordProcessed(participant, record, term_before)

    requires: participant.state = claiming

    -- Reading the claim back is what makes the verdict available, and the same act
    -- puts the claimant in possession of the prefix
    -- (AConferredClaimantHoldsThePrefix).
    requires: record.position.offset >= participant.claim.position.offset

    -- Nothing at or above this claim's term preceded it
    -- (TheLocalTestDecidesTheSameVerdict).
    requires: term_before < participant.claim.term

    ensures:
        -- The window this opens is what RecoverFollowerOnFailedTakeover covers.
        FollowerStopped(follower: participant.follower)

        participant.leader = LeaderPipeline.created(
            term: participant.claim.term,
            resume_from: participant.claim.position
        )
        participant.follower = null
        participant.claim = null
        participant.abandoned_claims = 0
        participant.state = leading
        participant.last_appended_at = now
}

rule RecoverFollowerOnFailedTakeover {
    -- Taking over failed after it had already stopped the follower.
    --   Re-open a running follower rather than leave the role backing a stopped one
    --   (NoRoleWithoutALivePipeline).
    --   Stopping the follower and deciding to recover have to be atomic with respect
    --   to whatever interrupted the takeover, so an interruption landing mid-teardown
    --   cannot leave the role backing a stopped pipeline either.
    when: TakeoverFailed(_, participant)

    requires: participant.state = claiming

    ensures:
        participant.follower = FollowerPipeline.created(
            resume_from: participant.replica_position
        )
        participant.claim = null
        -- The claim did come back and did confer, so reads are arriving; what failed
        -- was the swap. Nothing to back off from.
        participant.abandoned_claims = 0
        participant.state = following
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now
        participant.election_timeout =
            RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min, config.election_timeout_max)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 7. Leading, and standing down
--
-- What a leader owes while it holds the role, and the ways it gives it up: it is
-- superseded, it evicts itself, its pipeline stops, or its eligibility is withdrawn.
------------------------------------------------------------

contract LeadingDiscipline {
    @invariant ExactlyOneLeaderIsEverAcknowledged
        -- At most one leader's writes are ever acknowledged per database.
        --   Two nodes may briefly both believe they lead, and both append.
        --   Only the higher term's writes are acknowledged, because acknowledgement waits on reading the write back at the leader's own term -- and the superseded leader meets the higher-term record first.
        --   The lower-term records it left behind are discarded by every reader.
        --   The fence is the term stamp plus the read-back check.
        --   See live-index.allium, which owns the acknowledgement itself.

    @invariant AcknowledgementFollowsLogOrder
        -- A leader acknowledges its writes in log order.
        --   The premise ExactlyOneLeaderIsEverAcknowledged rests on: it guarantees a
        --   superseded leader reaches the record that supersedes it before it could
        --   acknowledge anything appended after it.

    @invariant AssertionOutpacesTheElectionTimeout
        -- A healthy leader's silence never reaches a follower's election timeout.
        --   By a margin that absorbs ordinary scheduling and delivery delay.
        --   Any append is an implicit assertion, so this only binds on an idle log.
        --   It binds on a BUSY leader too: the assertion must not queue behind the leader's own indexing work, or a leader falls silent exactly while it is doing the most, and followers read its silence as absence.
        --   The mirror of QuietIsMeasuredAcrossReads -- neither side may let its own load look like the other's failure.
}

config {
    -- How often a leader asserts leadership on a log with no other traffic. Well below
    -- election_timeout_min (section 5) -- see AssertionOutpacesTheElectionTimeout.
    assertion_interval: Duration = 1.second

    -- How long a leader keeps reading without its own appends coming back before it
    -- resigns.
    acknowledgement_timeout: Duration = 30.seconds
}

rule AssertLeadership {
    -- Followers time out on silence, so a leader with nothing to write says so.
    --   Only idle logs cost anything: every ordinary append is already an assertion.
    when: participant: Participant.last_appended_at + config.assertion_interval <= now

    requires: participant.state = leading

    ensures:
        AssertionAppended(log: participant.log, term: participant.leader.term)
        participant.last_appended_at = now
}

rule ResignOnSupersession {
    -- A higher term read back means another node's claim conferred leadership.
    --   This leader's outstanding writes will be discarded by every reader, so it
    --   resigns without acknowledging them (ExactlyOneLeaderIsEverAcknowledged) and
    --   follows on from where it had read to.
    when: RecordProcessed(participant, record, term_before)

    requires: participant.state = leading
    requires: record.term > participant.leader.term

    ensures:
        participant.follower = FollowerPipeline.created(
            resume_from: participant.replica_position
        )
        participant.leader = null
        participant.state = following
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now
        participant.election_timeout =
            RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min, config.election_timeout_max)
}

rule ResignOnReadBackStall {
    -- A leader that has been reading and is not getting its own appends back.
    --   The one failure no other node can resolve: it holds the term, so nothing it
    --   writes is superseded, and it goes on appending records every reader discards
    --   while asserting often enough that no follower times out. Nobody else will
    --   displace it, so it stands down and lets the election run.
    --   A leader reads its own assertions back as well as its client writes, so on any
    --   healthy leader this stopwatch is reset every assertion interval, whether or not
    --   there is any traffic.
    --   Measured across the leader's own reads, never against the clock, which would resign it for its own slowness (QuietIsMeasuredAcrossReads).
    when: participant: Participant.read_back_stalled

    requires: participant.state = leading

    ensures:
        participant.follower = FollowerPipeline.created(
            resume_from: participant.replica_position
        )
        participant.leader = null
        participant.state = following
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now
        participant.election_timeout =
            RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min, config.election_timeout_max)
}

rule ResignOnPipelineFailure {
    -- The leader pipeline stopped running while the participant still held the role.
    --   Without this the role outlives the pipeline backing it
    --   (NoRoleWithoutALivePipeline): the node goes on reporting itself leader and
    --   accepting client writes it can never acknowledge, and neither of the other
    --   exits can fire, because both need the leader to still be reading or appending.
    when: PipelineFailed(_, participant)

    requires: participant.state = leading

    ensures:
        participant.follower = FollowerPipeline.created(
            resume_from: participant.replica_position
        )
        participant.leader = null
        participant.state = following
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now
        participant.election_timeout =
            RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min, config.election_timeout_max)
}

rule FailDatabaseOnFollowerPipelineFailure {
    -- The follower pipeline stopped running while the participant was following or claiming.
    --   Reading the log is the only way the participant learns anything, so a dead follower freezes every threshold and the participant would sit wedged forever, in a state indistinguishable from a healthy quiet one.
    --   The database fails rather than the follower being replaced: the leader's exit (ResignOnPipelineFailure) hands the role to a healthier node, but a follower has nobody to hand its reading to on the node it serves.
    --   Tearing the database down tears the participant down with it (see Database).
    when: PipelineFailed(_, participant)

    requires: participant.state = following or participant.state = claiming

    ensures:
        DatabaseFailed(database: participant.database)
}

rule StandDownOnIneligibility {
    -- The deployment withdrew this node's eligibility to lead while it was leading.
    --   Draining a node before maintenance, so that it gives the role up rather than
    --   holding it until it is killed -- which would cost an election timeout of write
    --   unavailability instead of a handover.
    when: participant: Participant.database.may_lead becomes false

    requires: participant.state = leading

    ensures:
        participant.follower = FollowerPipeline.created(
            resume_from: participant.replica_position
        )
        participant.leader = null
        participant.state = following
        participant.last_read_at = now
        participant.last_record_at = now
        participant.election_timeout =
            RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min, config.election_timeout_max)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 8. What the node may rely on
--
-- Every property group this spec defines, gathered into the one surface the node's
-- query and submit paths face.
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Node {
    -- The node hosting this participant, and its query and submit paths.
    identified_by: NodeIdentity where name != ""
}

surface LeadershipRole {
    facing node: Node

    context participant: Participant

    exposes:
        participant.state

    provides:
        -- The node began participating in a database's leadership.
        ParticipantOpened(node, database)

        -- The log refused this participant's claim append, so nothing was written.
        ClaimAppendFailed(node, participant)

        -- Taking over did not complete: the node interrupted it, or a step of the
        -- handover failed.
        --   Only meaningful while the claim is outstanding, because after that the
        --   takeover has either finished or been abandoned.
        TakeoverFailed(node, participant) when participant.state = claiming

        -- A running pipeline stopped of its own accord.
        PipelineFailed(node, participant)

    contracts:
        fulfils SingleLogReader
        fulfils TermDiscipline
        fulfils ClaimDiscipline
        fulfils HandoverDiscipline
        fulfils LeadingDiscipline

    @guarantee LeadershipChangesWithoutARebuild
        -- A change of leader costs a change of role, never a re-read of the log.
        --   Every pipeline this spec opens resumes from the position the participant had already reached, in both directions, so neither resigning nor taking over re-reads anything.
        --   What local state survives the swap beyond that position is live-index.allium's to say; what this spec guarantees is that nothing has to be re-read to reconstruct it.
}

rule OpenParticipant {
    -- A node starts participating in a database's leadership, as a follower.
    --   Seeding the stopwatch from the current time and drawing a timeout up front is
    --   what stops a fresh participant claiming on its very first read. Without it
    --   every node in a cold-started cluster claims at once -- which is exactly the
    --   case the randomisation exists for.
    when: ParticipantOpened(node, database)

    ensures:
        participant = Participant.created(
            node: node,
            database: database,
            log: database.log,
            state: following,

            -- Durable below (TheTermIsDurableBelowAndMonotonicAbove).
            highest_term_seen: database.boundary.term,
            replica_position: database.boundary.position,

            last_read_at: now,
            last_record_at: now,
            abandoned_claims: 0,
            election_timeout:
                RandomTimeout(config.election_timeout_min, config.election_timeout_max),
            follower: FollowerPipeline.created(resume_from: database.boundary.position)
        )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 9. What the deployment must supply
--
-- The settings election depends on and cannot enforce for itself.
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Operator {
    -- Whoever deploys and configures the node and the log.
    identified_by: NodeIdentity where name != ""
}

surface DeploymentPolicy {
    facing operator: Operator

    context participant: Participant

    exposes:
        participant.database.may_lead

    @guarantee DurabilityComesFromDeployedConfiguration
        -- AcceptedRecordsAreNeverWithdrawn has to hold in the DEPLOYED configuration.
        --   Settings that let an accepted append be lost -- acknowledging before the
        --   record is replicated widely enough to survive a failure, or letting a
        --   replica that never received it take over -- would let two nodes reach
        --   opposite verdicts on the same election.
        --   Where the log can report its own settings, they are worth checking when the
        --   database first connects, alongside the other structural checks made there:
        --   caught at startup this is an error message, caught later it is split
        --   leadership.

    @guarantee RetentionRespectsTheBoundary
        -- Retention must not drop records at or after the persisted boundary.
        --   Cutting below it strands a restarting node and unmoors the term at once.

    @guarantee AnIdleLogStillTicks
        -- Empty reads have to be reported often enough to time an election by.
        --   QuietIsReported makes them happen at all; this fixes the rate. The
        --   stopwatch advances one reporting interval at a time, so that interval is
        --   the resolution of every timeout in this spec: comparable to the election
        --   timeout and the three timeouts cannot be told apart, and comparable to the
        --   spread between the minimum and maximum and the randomisation stops
        --   separating followers at all.
        --   It also bounds how long a dead leader goes unnoticed, since between reports
        --   no evidence of its silence reaches anybody.

    @guarantee AtLeastOneNodeMayLead
        -- Some participant in each database is eligible to lead.
        --   Nothing inside this spec can supply a leader if the deployment excludes
        --   every candidate, and nothing inside it reports the absence either: with no
        --   leader every node sits in following, which is also what a node looks like
        --   when some other node is leading perfectly well.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 10. Open questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Leadership balance across nodes. Randomised timeouts choose a leader but do not spread leadership: because leadership only moves when the incumbent fails or stops, an uneven distribution accumulates rather than averaging out, and a restarted node picks up no share of the load until an existing leader goes away. Deliberately out of scope while the databases per cluster number two or three. Withdrawing eligibility now stands a leader down (StandDownOnIneligibility), which covers draining a node before maintenance; a leader resigning as it shuts down would cover rolling restarts on the same mechanism. What signal should decide that balance has started to bite -- and is per-node leader count the right thing to balance, or is it load?"

open question "Election granularity beyond the database. This spec elects one leader per database, matching a replica log that is a single ordered sequence. If a database's log is later split into several independently ordered parts, each part is separately electable and a node could lead one and follow another. Is per-part election the intended model, or should a database keep a single leader across all its parts -- and if the latter, which part's ordering adjudicates the claim?"

open question "A leader that cannot make progress but reads and appends happily. Every self-eviction here turns on the leader's own reads and appends, so a leader partitioned from its source of work still asserts leadership, no follower times out, no election runs, and no client transaction gets through. The same shape covers a leader whose block flush fails permanently: it reads its own writes back, asserts on time, and cannot be displaced by anyone. Should a leader that cannot complete a cycle of work stand down, whatever the reason -- and how is that distinguished from a genuinely idle database?"

open question "A participant whose reads succeed but bring back nothing that exists. The stopwatch stops a participant that is not being read to from claiming, because nothing advances it -- but not one whose reads complete and are served nothing while records are in fact being appended: a lost partition assignment, a subscription to the wrong part of the log, a visibility split that reaches the reader and not the writer. Such a participant claims on a current term, evicts a healthy leader, then cannot read its own claim back and abandons, so the cost is a needless failover roughly every claim_timeout for as long as it stays broken -- bounded (ALoserCannotStarveTheElection) rather than prevented. The mechanism that would prevent it is a probe: a fresh reader started at the claimant's own position, an empty first read being the only honest answer to whether anything deliverable follows — it asks delivery about delivery, where a log's end position also counts positions a reader is never delivered. Not built, because the residual is narrow and a reader per election attempt buys little. What would tell us it had started to bite, given that a needless failover and a legitimate one look the same from every node?"

open question "Observing that a database has no leader. Every leaderless failure presents identically from outside -- every participant in following -- and that is also what a healthy database looks like from any node that is not its leader. So no node can tell locally whether the role is vacant, and none of AtLeastOneNodeMayLead unmet, a claimant whose reads have stopped, or a reader that has stopped reporting empty reads is visible as an absence of leadership. What should carry that signal: the log's own quiet, aggregated across nodes, or something a leader publishes whose absence is the alarm?"
